1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,046
A ruler, a kitchen scale, a stopwatch.

2
00:00:03,046 --> 00:00:04,732
Each has a smallest step.

3
00:00:05,132 --> 00:00:06,221
So, a puzzle.

4
00:00:06,221 --> 00:00:09,817
Can a ruler measure something
thinner than its smallest mark?

5
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:11,522
Aarav tries it.

6
00:00:11,522 --> 00:00:14,691
He holds his ruler
against one bicycle spoke.

7
00:00:15,041 --> 00:00:18,392
It sits between the two
and three millimetre marks.

8
00:00:18,392 --> 00:00:19,526
Two point one?

9
00:00:19,526 --> 00:00:20,710
Two point five?

10
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:22,255
The ruler cannot say.

11
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:26,157
By the end, you will read it to
one hundredth of a millimetre.

12
00:00:26,807 --> 00:00:28,927
The ruler's problem has a name.

13
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:29,901
Least count.

14
00:00:30,251 --> 00:00:34,055
It is the smallest value an
instrument can measure correctly.

15
00:00:34,505 --> 00:00:38,542
On a ruler, the smallest gap
between two marks is one millimetre.

16
00:00:38,542 --> 00:00:40,897
So its least count is one millimetre.

17
00:00:41,297 --> 00:00:45,348
A stopwatch showing hundredths:
zero point zero one second.

18
00:00:45,798 --> 00:00:48,428
A smaller least count
means a finer measurement.

19
00:00:49,078 --> 00:00:52,170
To beat the ruler, add
a second, sliding scale.

20
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:53,586
The vernier callipers.

21
00:00:53,936 --> 00:00:56,837
The main scale has marks
one millimetre apart.

22
00:00:57,187 --> 00:01:01,779
The sliding vernier scale has ten
marks, squeezed into nine millimetres.

23
00:01:02,229 --> 00:01:04,229
Close the jaws on the spoke.

24
00:01:04,229 --> 00:01:07,442
The vernier zero stops
just after two millimetres.

25
00:01:07,892 --> 00:01:12,298
The third vernier mark lines up
exactly with a main scale mark.

26
00:01:12,748 --> 00:01:15,939
So the reading is two
millimetres, plus three tenths.

27
00:01:15,939 --> 00:01:17,642
Two point three millimetres.

28
00:01:18,242 --> 00:01:19,245
Slow replay.

29
00:01:19,245 --> 00:01:24,093
Each time the jaw opens one tenth of
a millimetre, the matching mark moves

30
00:01:24,093 --> 00:01:24,993
one step.

31
00:01:25,390 --> 00:01:28,677
On the graph, the ruler jumps
in one millimetre steps.

32
00:01:28,677 --> 00:01:31,172
The vernier, in steps ten times smaller.

33
00:01:31,822 --> 00:01:32,886
Why one tenth?

34
00:01:32,886 --> 00:01:35,465
Let's work it out, one step at a time.

35
00:01:35,815 --> 00:01:36,738
Step one.

36
00:01:36,738 --> 00:01:40,597
Ten vernier divisions equal
nine main scale divisions.

37
00:01:41,347 --> 00:01:42,247
Step two.

38
00:01:42,227 --> 00:01:45,977
So one vernier division is
zero point nine millimetre.

39
00:01:46,727 --> 00:01:47,629
Step three.

40
00:01:47,629 --> 00:01:49,894
The least count is the difference.

41
00:01:49,894 --> 00:01:53,257
One main scale division,
minus one vernier division.

42
00:01:54,007 --> 00:01:54,973
Step four.

43
00:01:54,973 --> 00:01:58,972
One minus zero point nine is
zero point one millimetre.

44
00:01:59,722 --> 00:02:03,912
Reading equals main scale reading,
plus the matching mark number times

45
00:02:03,912 --> 00:02:05,108
the least count.

46
00:02:05,808 --> 00:02:06,763
Remember this.

47
00:02:06,763 --> 00:02:10,532
You can never read finer than the
least count of your instrument.

48
00:02:11,232 --> 00:02:12,312
Side by side.

49
00:02:12,312 --> 00:02:15,031
The ruler's least count is one millimetre.

50
00:02:15,031 --> 00:02:17,909
The spoke reads only
between two and three.

51
00:02:18,359 --> 00:02:21,709
The vernier's least count is
zero point one millimetre.

52
00:02:21,709 --> 00:02:24,127
The same spoke reads two point three.

53
00:02:24,727 --> 00:02:25,752
Now you try.

54
00:02:25,752 --> 00:02:30,271
Can a millimetre ruler tell a two
point three millimetre spoke from a two

55
00:02:30,271 --> 00:02:31,187
point four?

56
00:02:31,187 --> 00:02:32,461
Pause and decide.

57
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:36,561
No!

58
00:02:36,139 --> 00:02:38,709
Both sit between the same two marks.

59
00:02:38,709 --> 00:02:42,610
Zero point one millimetre is
below the ruler's least count.

60
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:45,500
For finer work, use a screw gauge.

61
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:49,943
One full turn of the cap moves the
screw a fixed distance, the pitch.

62
00:02:49,943 --> 00:02:51,522
Here, half a millimetre.

63
00:02:51,972 --> 00:02:55,391
The cap has fifty equal
divisions around its edge.

64
00:02:55,791 --> 00:03:00,209
So one division moves the screw
by half a millimetre over fifty.

65
00:03:00,209 --> 00:03:02,433
Zero point zero one millimetre.

66
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:08,251
Least count equals the pitch, divided by
the number of circular scale divisions.

67
00:03:08,901 --> 00:03:10,030
Side by side.

68
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:13,253
The vernier reads to zero
point one millimetre.

69
00:03:13,253 --> 00:03:16,213
The screw gauge, to zero point zero one.

70
00:03:16,663 --> 00:03:17,563
Your turn.

71
00:03:17,456 --> 00:03:21,811
To measure one sheet of paper, about
zero point one millimetre thick, which

72
00:03:21,811 --> 00:03:22,886
would you pick?

73
00:03:26,086 --> 00:03:27,215
The screw gauge!

74
00:03:27,215 --> 00:03:30,381
On the vernier, the sheet
is just one least count.

75
00:03:30,381 --> 00:03:32,601
The screw gauge splits it into ten.

76
00:03:33,251 --> 00:03:34,345
Worked example.

77
00:03:34,345 --> 00:03:36,661
The spoke goes in the screw gauge.

78
00:03:37,011 --> 00:03:40,276
Pitch zero point five
millimetre, fifty divisions.

79
00:03:40,276 --> 00:03:44,890
The main scale shows two millimetres,
and division thirty one lines up.

80
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:48,318
Least count, zero point five over fifty.

81
00:03:48,318 --> 00:03:50,538
Zero point zero one millimetre.

82
00:03:51,288 --> 00:03:54,962
Circular part, thirty one
times zero point zero one.

83
00:03:54,962 --> 00:03:57,225
Zero point three one millimetre.

84
00:03:57,975 --> 00:04:00,435
Total, two point three one millimetres.

85
00:04:00,435 --> 00:04:02,478
One more digit than the vernier.

86
00:04:03,128 --> 00:04:04,594
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:04,944 --> 00:04:09,347
Many students write the vernier least
count as one, divided by the number

88
00:04:09,347 --> 00:04:10,771
of vernier divisions.

89
00:04:11,171 --> 00:04:15,341
That works only when a main
scale division is one millimetre.

90
00:04:15,341 --> 00:04:18,858
Always use one main scale
division, divided by N.

91
00:04:18,858 --> 00:04:23,744
Half a millimetre over twenty five is
zero point zero two, not zero point

92
00:04:23,744 --> 00:04:24,660
zero four.

93
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:26,685
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:26,685 --> 00:04:27,966
JEE Main and NEET.

95
00:04:27,966 --> 00:04:32,144
Find the least count, or the
reading, of a vernier or screw gauge.

96
00:04:32,744 --> 00:04:34,043
Exam level now.

97
00:04:34,043 --> 00:04:38,477
Twenty vernier divisions match
nineteen half millimetre divisions.

98
00:04:38,477 --> 00:04:42,252
Main scale twelve point
five, eighth mark coincides.

99
00:04:42,252 --> 00:04:43,605
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:50,394
The answer is B.

101
00:04:50,394 --> 00:04:52,925
Twelve point seven zero millimetres.

102
00:04:53,425 --> 00:04:57,120
Least count is zero point
five divided by twenty.

103
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,868
Zero point zero two five millimetre.

104
00:05:00,368 --> 00:05:02,891
Eight times that is zero point two.

105
00:05:02,891 --> 00:05:04,549
Add twelve point five.

106
00:05:04,549 --> 00:05:06,999
Twelve point seven zero millimetres.

107
00:05:07,599 --> 00:05:09,064
Here is your recap.

108
00:05:09,064 --> 00:05:10,568
Screenshot this card.

109
00:05:13,768 --> 00:05:16,605
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

110
00:05:17,005 --> 00:05:17,905
One.

111
00:05:17,513 --> 00:05:21,464
Why can a ruler not read a two
point three millimetre spoke?

112
00:05:22,364 --> 00:05:23,264
Two.

113
00:05:22,884 --> 00:05:25,803
Ten vernier divisions
equal nine millimetres.

114
00:05:25,803 --> 00:05:27,589
What is the least count?

115
00:05:28,489 --> 00:05:29,389
Three.

116
00:05:29,067 --> 00:05:31,824
Pitch one millimetre,
one hundred divisions.

117
00:05:31,824 --> 00:05:33,483
What is the least count?

118
00:05:34,582 --> 00:05:39,412
Next time: one glass of water, measured
five times, gives five numbers.

119
00:05:39,412 --> 00:05:40,594
Which is right?

120
00:05:40,594 --> 00:05:43,299
Absolute, relative and percentage errors.
